ged and move\n   686\t   :next past the member, so the same condition is reported again on that\n   687\t   member's next turn; nothing is auto-resynchronized. A defect carries the\n   688\t   raw read under :result exactly as the step classified it -- a transport\n   689\t   that answered with something that is not a well-formed outcome map is\n   690\t   reported with its answer retained, never folded into a meaning nobody\n   691\t   chose. A refused read reports the same way under its own outcome: a\n   692\t   policy declined the member's read, nothing was observed, and the member\n   693\t   keeps its cursor -- and an unrecognized outcome reports this way too,\n   694\t   a newer contract being a refusal, not a defect. Blocked and\n   695\t   ended members never prevent later members from being checked, and a\n   696\t   member that yielded a payload loses its turn, so a continuously ready\n   697\t   member cannot starve another.\n   698\t\n   699\t   The effect is materialize!, which answers ok or throws: :failed is\n   700\t   unreachable, and a throwing effect propagates before any cursor moves,\n   701\t   so the caller's state is untouched and the same payload is reprocessed\n   702\t   from the same cursor once the backend succeeds.\"\n   703\t  [handle state]\n   704\t  (let [n (count (:members state))]\n   705\t    (if (zero? n)\n   706\t      {:state state, :signal :dao.stream/blocked}\n   707\t      (let [effect (fn [payload]\n   708\t                     {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n   709\t                      :address (materialize! handle payload)})]\n   710\t        (loop [i (:next state)\n  1220\t  (let [batch (encoder/program-batch ast)\n  1221\t        {:keys [tree error log]}\n  1222\t        (macro/expand-batch batch (get-in state [:expander :ctx]))]\n  1223\t    (str \"AST:\\n\" (format-value ast)\n  1224\t         \"\\n\\nInput rows:\\n\" (format-value batch)\n  1225\t         (if tree\n  1226\t           (str \"\\n\\nExpanded rows:\\n\" (format-value tree))\n  1227\t           (str \"\\n\\nExpansion error:\\n\" (format-value error)))\n  1228\t         \"\\n\\nEvents:\\n\" (format-value (second log)))))\n  1229\t\n  1230\t\n  1231\t(defn- rebuild-session\n  1232\t  \"Replace the VM, the expander, and their media, attachments, and\n  1233\t   observers.  The value history is cleared with them: a closure in `*1`\n  1234\t   names a code segment the old VM held, which the new one does not.  The\n  1235\t   new expander holds only the standard forms.  A require still pending\n  1236\t   on the old VM is dropped with it: its link pair is the session's, and\n  1237\t   the new session answers from its own.\"\n  1238\t  [state vm-type]\n  1239\t  (merge state\n  1240\t         (make-session vm-type (:output-stream state)\n  1241\t                       (:extra-primitives state)\n  1242\t                       (:shell-token state)\n  1243\t                       (:index-store state))\n  1244\t         {:vm-type vm-type\n  1245\t          :ingress-loss? false\n  1246\t          :last-value nil\n  1247\t          :last-value-2 nil\n  1248\t          :last-value-3 nil\n  1249\t          :pending-run nil}))\n  1250\t\n  1251\t\n  1252\t(declare repl-state)\n  1253\t\n  1254\t\n  1255\t(defn handle-command\n  1256\t  \"Answer one shell command.  Unsupported arguments are answered as text, not\n  1257\t   thrown, so a mistyped command cannot end a step.\"\n  1258\t  [state form]\n  1259\t  (let [[command & args] form]\n  1260\t    (case command\n  1261\t      vm (let [vm-type (first args)]\n  1262\t           (if (contains? vm-constructors vm-type)\n  1263\t             [(rebuild-session state vm-type)\n  1264\t              (str \"Switched to \" (get vm-labels vm-type) \" (store cleared)\")]\n  1265\t             [state (str \"Error: Unknown Yin REPL VM type \" (pr-str vm-type)\n  1266\t                         \"; supported: \"\n  1267\t                         (pr-str (vec (keys vm-constructors))))]))